# sprint_sec_fs_native.oot — SEC hostile audit of native fs_* + sh mkdir/copy/clean
# Date: 2026-08-14
# Role: SEC. This file only. No product edit. Did not touch fs.oo / sh.oo / chs_rt_fs.c.
# Marker: post-DE10 partial native land (mkdir_p + copy_tree); clean still shell
# Surfaces: /home/jeryd/Projects/openOODA/ooda/std/os/fs.oo
#           /home/jeryd/Projects/openOODA/ooda/std/sh.oo
#           /home/jeryd/Projects/openOODA/ooda/runtime/chs_rt_fs.c
#           /home/jeryd/Projects/openOODA/ooda/std/os/process.oo
#           /home/jeryd/Projects/openOODA/ooda/oodac/check_cap_util.oo
# Hunt: mkdir_p ignore fs_mkdir Err (TOCTOU / file-in-the-way)
#       copy recursion
#       clean still rm -rf injection if arg gate bypassed
#       OODA_FS_WRITEDIR still required by runtime
# Method: static re-read. No live rm -rf / mkdir-race probe this turn.
#         Do not claim secure. Do not green the native wave.

========================================================================
VERDICT
========================================================================

**Not sealed. Partial move only. Residual honesty.**

`sh_mkdir_p` is now `&FsWriteCap` → `fs_mkdir_p`.
`sh_copy` is now `&FsReadCap` + `&FsWriteCap` → `fs_copy_tree`.
Those two left `sh -c`. That is real.

They did not become a seal.

`fs_mkdir_p` **never returns Err**. Every `fs_mkdir` Result is `let _ =`.
Writedir deny, EEXIST-as-file, ENOTDIR, EACCES, and the last-component
fail are all discarded. Callers see `Ok(void)`. `fs_copy_tree`'s
`if mk.is_err()` is dead.

`fs_copy_tree` follows `stat`/`opendir` (no `lstat`). A symlink in
`src` walks any readable host tree. Recursion has no depth cap and
no cycle stop. A link back to `src` is unbounded. `fs_is_dir` clamps
to 1024 bytes; writes clamp to `PATH_MAX`. Those are not the same
path.

`sh_clean` is still `ProcessCap` + `process_exec("rm -rf " + dir)`.
`OODA_FS_WRITEDIR` does not apply. The char gate is not argv. Space
and `-` are in-class. `--no-preserve-root /` **passes** `sh_arg_ok`.
If the gate is bypassed, `;` `|` `$()` are the old RCE. Even with
the gate, GNU `rm` can take a second operand or a wipe flag.

Runtime mutate still **requires** `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR` (unset / empty
/ `/` deny). Native mkdir/write/remove/link/symlink all call
`oo_process_policy_getenv("OODA_FS_WRITEDIR")`. The facade hides
that fail: `fs_mkdir_p` swallows the deny and still returns Ok.
`sh_clean` never consults the overlay.

Do not say native fs replaced shell. Do not say mkdir_p is mkdir -p.
Do not say copy is cp -a. Do not say clean is gated.

========================================================================
WHAT LANDED (do not over-claim)
========================================================================

Re-read this turn.

| Op | Tip claim | Live | Cap |
|----|-----------|------|-----|
| `sh_mkdir_p` | native | `return fs_mkdir_p(fw, dir)` after `sh_arg_ok` | **FsWriteCap** |
| `sh_copy` | native | `return fs_copy_tree(fr, fw, src, dst)` after gate | **FsReadCap + FsWriteCap** |
| `sh_clean` | native residual | `process_exec(p, "rm -rf " + dir)` | **ProcessCap** |
| `fs_mkdir_p` | prefixes | walk `/` + `let _ = fs_mkdir` + **always Ok** | Fw |
| `fs_copy_file` | one file | `read_file` whole → `write_file` | Fr+Fw |
| `fs_copy_tree` | walk + mkdir + copy | `fs_is_dir` then recurse `src+"/"+name` | Fr+Fw |

`sh.oo:15` still says "git/tar/chmod/clean still shell after sh_arg_ok
(no rmdir builtin)". That comment is true. mkdir/copy comments in the
header (beats L8) are stale: they still list mkdir/copy as path mutators
next to chmod as if they were the same class.

No `remove_tree` / `rmdir` builtin. That is why clean stayed shell.

========================================================================
HUNT 1 — fs_mkdir_p ignores fs_mkdir Err (TOCTOU / file-in-the-way)
========================================================================

Proof of shape (`std/os/fs.oo:102-135`):

    // mkdir every prefix. Existing dirs: fs_mkdir may Err; we continue.
    pub fn fs_mkdir_p(fw: &FsWriteCap, path: String) -> Result[Void, String]
    {
        ...
                    let _ = fs_mkdir(fw, acc);
        ...
            let _ = fs_mkdir(fw, acc);
        ...
        return Ok(void);
    }

There is **no** `is_err` branch. There is **no** `fs_is_dir` confirm
after a failed mkdir. EEXIST-as-dir and EEXIST-as-file are the same
discard. Writedir deny is the same discard.

Runtime `oo_fs_mkdir` (`chs_rt_fs.c:133-141`):

    require_fswrite
    OODA_FS_WRITEDIR fail-closed (unset / empty / `/` / not under)
    mkdir(cpath, 0777) == 0  → Ok
    else                     → Err("fs_mkdir failed")

EEXIST is **not** success. POSIX `mkdir(2)` fails if the last
component exists, dir or not. The .oo layer treats that Err as
"probably already a dir." That is the mkdir -p happy path **and**
the file-in-the-way lie.

### File in the way

1. Prefix is a **regular file**.
   `mkdir(prefix)` → EEXIST → discarded.
   `mkdir(prefix/child)` → ENOTDIR → discarded.
   `fs_mkdir_p` → **Ok(void)**.
   Dest tree does not exist.

2. Prefix is a **symlink to a file**.
   Same. `stat` later (copy) may disagree with what mkdir created.

3. Last component is a **file**, caller wanted a directory.
   `mkdir` fails. `fs_mkdir_p` still Ok. `fs_copy_tree` then writes
   `dst/name` via `write_file`. `writedir_open_trunc` does
   `open(parent, O_DIRECTORY)` — parent is a file → fail. Copy of
   children fails **later**, or returns Ok on an empty dirent list
   if the src walk is also confused. The mkdir_p Result already lied.

### TOCTOU (check vs create)

`oo_fs_mkdir` is not `mkdirat`. Order is:

    path_under_writedir(cpath)     // realpath of path, or parent
    mkdir(cpath, 0777)             // follows symlink components

No `O_NOFOLLOW`. No dirfd pin. Contrast `write_file`, which uses
`openat(..., O_NOFOLLOW)` after parent realpath (`chs_rt_fs.c:33-39`).

Race:

    path = $WRITEDIR/subdir/new
    parent $WRITEDIR/subdir is a real directory → allow
    attacker replaces subdir with a symlink to /etc
    mkdir("$WRITEDIR/subdir/new") follows the link → /etc/new

`path_under_writedir` for a **new** leaf uses `realpath(parent)`.
That is a TOCTOU window, not a seal. Same class as T3 W8 (parent
dentry rename) plus a follow-symlink create that write_file
partially closed at the leaf.

If the **new** path's parent is already a symlink whose realpath
leaves writedir, the check denies. That is the static case. The
swap-after-check case is open.

### Swallowing the overlay

`fs_mkdir("/tmp/outside")` with `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR=/tmp/wd` is Err.
`fs_mkdir_p("/tmp/outside/a/b")` is Ok. Every prefix mkdir denied.
Caller proceeds as if the tree exists.

`fs_mkdir_p("rel/path")` is Ok. Runtime requires `path[0]=='/'`
(`path_under_writedir` L26). Relative mkdir is always denied. Facade
hides it.

`fs_mkdir_p("/")` skips (`acc != "/"`). Ok. No-op.

`fs_copy_tree` (`fs.oo:155-156`):

    let mk = fs_mkdir_p(fw, dst);
    if mk.is_err() { return mk; }

Dead. mkdir_p cannot take that branch.

| Case | mkdir(2) / policy | mkdir_p Result | Honest? |
|------|-------------------|----------------|---------|
| new dir under writedir | 0 | Ok | yes |
| existing dir | EEXIST | Ok | intended mkdir -p |
| existing **file** | EEXIST | **Ok** | **NO** |
| parent is file | ENOTDIR | **Ok** | **NO** |
| outside writedir | policy Err | **Ok** | **NO** |
| relative path | policy Err | **Ok** | **NO** |
| parent swapped to symlink | mkdir follows | Ok if create works | **TOCTOU** |
| EACCES / EROFS | Err | **Ok** | **NO** |

Fix that would be honesty, not this turn: on mkdir Err, `lstat`;
if not `S_ISDIR`, return that Err. Never discard writedir deny.
Use `mkdirat` + `O_NOFOLLOW` / `AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW`. Do not
claim mkdir -p until EEXIST-dir is distinguished.

========================================================================
HUNT 2 — copy recursion
========================================================================

`fs_copy_file` (`fs.oo:137-148`): whole-file `fs_read_file` then
`fs_write_file`. No dest-parent create. No mode. No symlink preserve.
Read Err is collapsed to `"fs_copy_file: read failed"` (lossy).
Match after `is_err` still binds `""` on the dead Err arm.

`fs_copy_tree` (`fs.oo:150-169`):

    if fs_is_dir(fr, src) {
        fs_mkdir_p(fw, dst);          // never errs
        for name in fs_read_dir(fr, src) {
            fs_copy_tree(..., src+"/"+name, dst+"/"+name);
        }
        return Ok(void);
    }
    return fs_copy_file(...);

`sh_copy` is this function plus `sh_arg_ok` on both strings.

### Follows symlinks

`oo_fs_is_dir` (`chs_rt_fs.c:117-122`): `stat`, not `lstat`.
`oo_fs_read_dir`: `opendir`, follows a dir symlink.
`oo_read_file`: `fopen(path.data)` — follows a file symlink.
Reads are **not** under `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR`.

Plant `src/leak → /etc` (dir) or `src/shadow → /etc/shadow` (file).
`fs_copy_tree` / `sh_copy` copies host bytes into `dst` (writes still
need writedir). FsReadCap is any-path read. This is copy-out, not
`cp -P`.

`readdir` skips `.` and `..` (`chs_rt_fs.c:107`). Names do not
contain `/`. Traversal is the symlink, not `../` in a dirent.

### No cycle / no depth

`src/loop → src` (or `src/up → src/..`):

    copy_tree(src)           // is_dir
      copy_tree(src/loop)    // is_dir via stat follow
        copy_tree(src/loop/loop)
          ...

No visited-inode set. No depth cap. Same class as `fs_walk`
(`fs.oo:50-76`, already residual). Recursion is host stack, not a
heap worklist. Deep or cyclic trees: stack smash / unbounded
mkdir+write under dest.

`fs_walk` residual is now also a **write** residual: copy_tree
mkdir_p + write_file each hop.

### is_dir 1024 vs write PATH_MAX

`oo_fs_is_dir` `to_cpath(..., 1024)`.
`oo_fs_mkdir` / `oo_write_file` `to_cpath(..., PATH_MAX)`.
`to_cpath` truncates silently (sprint12 already named this).

A src path ≥1024 bytes can classify a **prefix** as dir/not-dir.
Copy then mkdir/write a longer (or different) string. Classification
and mutate are not the same object.

### Recursion + swallowed mkdir_p

If `dst` mkdir_p lied (file in the way / outside writedir / relative):

- src dir + dest not actually a dir → child writes `dst/name` fail
  or (if dest is a symlink dir outside) `path_under_writedir` on
  the child may deny — or, if dest realpath is still under writedir,
  write lands in an unexpected real directory.
- src dir + dest missing → `write_file` parent realpath fails → Err
  on the first file. Empty src dir: **Ok** after a failed mkdir_p.
  Silent no-op success.

### TOCTOU on the is_dir fork

    if fs_is_dir(src) { read_dir / recurse }
    else              { copy_file }

Swap src dir→file between `is_dir` and `opendir`: empty list, Ok.
Swap file→dir between `is_dir` and `fopen`: read fails, Err.
Swap file→symlink-to-secret between `is_dir` and `fopen`: copy-out.

Not a check-then-openat. Two independent path lookups.

### Memory / shape

Whole file in one `String`. No size cap. Large file is host-heap DoS
under Fr+Fw. Not sendfile. NULs: `oo_read_file` keeps `len`; write
uses `content.len`. Adjacent: `buf[n]=0` after `oo_str_alloc_payload(sz)`
when `n==sz` is one-past (chs_rt_fs.c:47-51). Not owned this hunt.

No native copy of fifo/socket/device. `fopen`/`stat` decide. A fifo
`fopen` blocks. Residual hang, not a cap hole.

`sh_copy` adds `sh_arg_ok`. Direct `fs_copy_tree` has **no** char
gate. Product API is the ungated recurse.

| Vector | Path-A want | Live |
|--------|-------------|------|
| symlink dir in src | copy link or refuse | **follow + recurse** |
| symlink file in src | copy link or refuse | **follow + read** |
| cycle link | refuse / cap | **unbounded recurse** |
| depth | cap | **none** |
| dest mkdir fail | Err | **Ok then later fail/no-op** |
| dest file-in-the-way | Err | **mkdir_p Ok** |
| is_dir vs mutate race | one lookup | **two lookups** |
| path clamp | deny long | **1024 vs PATH_MAX truncate** |
| relative dest | deny or cwd policy | mkdir deny + **Ok** facade |
| metadata / mode | document | **dropped** (0666 write leaf) |

Do not call this `cp -r`. It is follow-everything recurse + lie-Ok
mkdir + whole-file rewrite.

========================================================================
HUNT 3 — sh_clean still rm -rf; injection if gate bypassed
========================================================================

Live (`sh.oo:38-45`):

    pub fn sh_clean(p: &ProcessCap, dir: String) -> Result[Void, String]
        requires chars_len(dir) > 0
    {
        if !sh_arg_ok(dir) { return Err("sh: rejected unsafe arg"); }
        let r = process_exec(p, "rm -rf " + dir);
        ...
    }

`process_exec` → `sys_exec(p, "sh", "-c", shell_cmd)` (`process.oo:34-38`).
Child env is DE1.8 (`OODA_*`/`OO_*` + `PATH=/usr/bin:/bin`). That is
not a quote. `shell_cmd` is one string. `rm` sees whatever `sh` word-splits.

No FsWriteCap. No writedir. No `unlinkat`. No `remove_tree`.
`fs_remove_file` is `unlink` of one leaf under writedir only
(`chs_rt_fs.c:123-131`). It cannot replace `rm -rf`.

### Gate (what it actually is)

`sh_arg_ok` (`sh.oo:21-34`): whitelist

    A-Za-z0-9 / . - _ = : + space

Reject: `;|&$`(){}<> \n\r'"\\` and anything else (incl. `* ? [ ] ~`
and non-ASCII; `char_at` is a UTF-8 scalar, `char_is_alpha` is
`len==1 && isalpha`).

Empty string: loop does not run → **true**. `requires chars_len(dir)>0`
is the empty check, if emit lowers it (`c_emit_contract.oo`). Do not
treat `requires` as a second security gate on the interpreter path
until a prove says so.

Smoke (`scripts/sh_arg_ok_smoke.oo`) only refuses `;` `&&` `$(`.
It does **not** refuse space, `-`, `=`, `../`, `/`.

### In-class (gate not bypassed — still destuctive)

These **pass** `sh_arg_ok`:

| `dir` | `sh -c` line | Effect |
|-------|--------------|--------|
| `/` | `rm -rf /` | GNU rm refuses without `--no-preserve-root`; still a wipe attempt |
| `--no-preserve-root /` | `rm -rf --no-preserve-root /` | **GNU wipe** |
| `foo /` | `rm -rf foo /` | second operand is `/` |
| `foo /tmp` | `rm -rf foo /tmp` | two trees |
| `-rf /home` | `rm -rf -rf /home` | extra flags + path |
| `--one-file-system x` | flag injection | tool semantics |
| `../../` | `rm -rf ../../` | cwd-relative climb |
| `/tmp/wd/../outside` | climb | **not** writedir-bound |

Space is the multi-argv hole. `-` is the option hole. `/` and `..`
are the path hole. The class was built so `+x`, `https://`, and
`-d /tmp/foo -t x` keep compiling. It cannot be a single-path seal.

`sh_clean` is the worst sink left: the prefix is already `-rf`.

Live caller shape (not an inject prove; shows ProcessCap + path
concat still hits this function):

    scripts/ooda_emit_health.oo:56  sh_clean(p, err_base + "*.err")
    → `*` **rejected** now (FP vs old glob clean)

    scripts/fs_writedir_smoke.oo:70  sh_clean(p, wd)
    → if `wd` is an absolute `/tmp/...` string, **allowed**,
      and delete is **not** limited to OODA_FS_WRITEDIR

### If the gate is bypassed

Bypass = `sh_arg_ok` not called, or a string that should fail
reaches `process_exec`. Then the pre-DE1 matrix is live again:

| Payload | Line | Result |
|---------|------|--------|
| `x; id` | `rm -rf x; id` | **RCE** |
| `x && id` | `&&` | **RCE** (`&` not in class; only if bypass) |
| `x\|id` | pipe | **RCE** |
| `x$(id)` | subst | **RCE** |
| `x`id`` | backticks | **RCE** |
| `x $OODA_FS_WRITEDIR` | `$` | env expand in **sh**, not in child `printenv` |
| `x\nid` | newline | second command |
| `*` / `/*` | glob | `rm -rf /*` if shell globbing |

`oo_sys_exec` does not parse `shell_cmd`. Fork+execvp of
`sh -c <string>`. Parent IFS/PATH/LD_* are stripped. That does
not stop metacharacters **inside** the string.

Bypass routes (honesty, not a live probe):

1. Call `process_exec` / `sys_exec` directly with the same concat.
2. Future edit drops the `if !sh_arg_ok` and keeps the concat.
3. `sh_arg_ok` on a different string than the one concatenated
   (not today's code; one `dir`).
4. Interpreter / seed path that does not emit `requires` and
   somehow skips the `if` — not claimed; not disproved here.

The gate is a filter in front of a shell. It is not argv. It is
not a cap. Bypass restores Critical injection. **Non-bypass
already has wipe-class option injection.**

git / tar / chmod remain shell on the same gate (`sh.oo:62-99`).
Out of this hunt except as proof the class was never a tool seal.
See `audit/sprint_sec_sh.oot`.

========================================================================
HUNT 4 — OODA_FS_WRITEDIR still required by runtime
========================================================================

Still the write overlay. Not removed. Not optional. Fail-closed.

| C op | getenv | Deny when |
|------|--------|-----------|
| `oo_write_file` | `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR` | unset / empty / `/` / not under (`chs_rt_fs.c:57-59`) |
| `oo_fs_remove_file` | same | L127-129 |
| `oo_fs_mkdir` | same | L137-139 |
| `oo_fs_hardlink` | same on **old and new** | L147-148 |
| `oo_fs_symlink` | same on **link and target** | L156-157 |

Policy read is `oo_process_policy_getenv` — key must be `OODA_` /
`OO_`. `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR` matches. A renamed `FS_WRITEDIR` would
be NULL → deny all. Good fail-closed.

`path_under_writedir` (`chs_rt_fs.c:24-32`):

- both path and dir must be absolute
- dir `/` refused
- `realpath(dir)` must work (writedir must exist)
- existing path: `realpath(path)` prefix+boundary
- missing leaf: parent realpath + basename not `.`/`..`/`/`

`write_file` then `openat(O_NOFOLLOW)`. **mkdir / unlink / link /
symlink do not.** Overlay is necessary and insufficient.

### What still needs the env

Every native mutate used by this wave:

    fs_mkdir / fs_mkdir_p / sh_mkdir_p
    fs_write_file / fs_copy_file / fs_copy_tree / sh_copy
    fs_remove_file
    fs_hardlink / fs_symlink

Without the env, each `oo_*` returns Err `… denied: path not under
OODA_FS_WRITEDIR` (mkdir/write/remove wording). Scripts that only
hold FsWriteCap cannot write. That is the T3 6.4 contract. It is
still there.

### What the env does **not** cover

| Surface | Writedir? |
|---------|-----------|
| `read_file` / `path_exists` / `file_size` | **no** — any-path `fopen(path.data)` |
| `fs_read_dir` / `fs_is_dir` / `fs_walk` | **no** |
| `fs_copy_tree` **src** | **no** (read side) |
| `sh_clean` `rm -rf` | **no** |
| `sh_chmod` / git / tar / find / curl | **no** |
| `process_exec` generally | **no** |

So: native mkdir/copy **still need** the env to succeed at C.
`fs_mkdir_p` **hides** the need (Ok on deny). `sh_clean` **ignores**
the need (shell delete anywhere uid can unlink).

Hardlink residual (T3 W7) is still open: hardlink an outside file
into writedir, then `write_file` / copy dest overwrites the outside
inode. `path_under_writedir` on the writedir leaf is true.

### Check map vs runtime

`sealed_fs_kind_of` still only names

    read_file / fs_read / path_exists / file_size
    write_file / fs_write

It does **not** name `oo_fs_mkdir`, `fs_mkdir`, `oo_fs_remove_file`,
`oo_fs_read_dir`, `oo_fs_is_dir`, `oo_fs_hardlink`, `oo_fs_symlink`.

Runtime still `require_fswrite` / `require_fsread` on those C ops.
Check-path seal for the new names is a **gap**. User wrappers take
typed caps, so `fs_mkdir_p(fw, …)` still needs a Fw argument to
typecheck the call. A direct `oo_fs_mkdir` free call is not
preferred-kind denied the way `write_file` is. Runtime exits 1 on
a forged/missing token (`oo_cap_require_fswrite`). Do not call the
check map complete for native mkdir/copy.

CAPS.oot still says "FsWriteCap — write_file. Writes also obey
OODA_FS_WRITEDIR". mkdir/remove/copy are not in that sentence.
Doc lag, not a runtime drop.

